.TH P256TEST 1
.SH NAME
p256test \- check p256(2) against another library, and time it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p256test
[
.B -s
.I secs
]
[
.I reps
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I P256test
checks
.IR p256 (2)
\(em the NIST curve arithmetic this TLS client verifies certificate
chains with \(em against vectors produced by OpenSSL 3.6.3, and reports
how long a verification takes.
.PP
The vectors are two key pairs per curve, the ECDH shared secret each
way, and an ECDSA signature over a fixed message with the digest
matched to the curve: SHA-256 for P-256 and SHA-384 for P-384.  Both
curves are checked because a certificate chain in the wild has a P-256
leaf under a P-384 intermediate, so a client that could only do one of
them would fail on real traffic.
.PP
Every positive check has a negative control beside it: the same
verification with one byte changed in the hash, in
.IR r ,
in
.IR s ,
or under the wrong public key, and a point moved off the curve.  A
verifier that answers yes to everything passes the positive checks
alone, so the controls are what makes the positives mean anything.
.PP
.I Reps
sets how many verifications and agreements the timing does; one by
default.  The timing is the reason to keep this command: certificate
verification is where a handshake spends its time, and this says what
one costs on the machine in hand.  Run it interpreted \(em
.B emu
.B -c0
\(em for a figure that means anything about a native kernel, which has
no JIT.
.PP
The last section prices the field operations themselves.  Each one is a
call out of interpreted Dis into
.IR libmp ,
so what a point formula costs is decided by how many of those crossings
it makes rather than by how many multiplications it does.  It reports
the best of five passes of four thousand calls: on an idle machine a
256-bit multiply alone is about 5 \(*ms, a multiply followed by a
reduction about 10, the single
.B mulmod
about 6, and an inversion about 19, and all four rise together when the
machine is busy \(em so read the differences rather than the figures.
The last ratio is the one that matters \(em an inversion costs between
two and a half and three modular multiplies here, not the thirty or
hundred the textbook formulas assume, which is why
.IR p256 (2)
stays in affine coordinates.  The section also checks that
.BR mulmod ,
.B addmod
and
.B submod
agree with the two-call forms, including a subtraction that goes
negative before it is reduced.
.PP
.B -s
turns the command into a workload as well as a test.  After the checks
it repeats a verification and an agreement for
.I secs
seconds, printing
.B P256-CYCLE
with each pass's cost, between
.B P256-SOAK-START
and
.BR P256-SOAK-END .
A serial log taken from outside can count the cycles, which is what
makes this usable as the non-chantest workload
.IR doc/merge-parallelvm-cloud.ms
asks for: channel traffic is the case a parallel interpreter is best
at, and this is allocation and module calls instead.  The controls stay
inside the loop \(em a pass whose verification stops verifying, or whose
agreement stops matching the vector, fails the command rather than
being counted.  Hosted and interpreted on an Apple laptop it manages
about 69 cycles in six seconds.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/p256test.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR chainbench (1),
.IR p256 (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
The vectors are compiled in.  Refreshing them means running OpenSSL
again and editing the constants at the top of the source.
